# Tacet Grammar (ISO EBNF)

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The grammar encodes STRUCTURE ONLY. It is deliberately permissive: it accepts a
# superset of valid programs. Fixity comes from spacing; blocks from indentation.
# Associativity, witness discharge, closure resolution, term/type distinction,
# and every other semantic fact are deferred to the analyser. Nothing here
# interprets --- the parse produces flat runs; the analyser folds them into trees.

# ── Token kinds ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   _         significant whitespace:       one or more spaces, no tabs
#   TACET     the sole keyword (reserved):  tacet
#   TYPE_ID   uppercase-initial ident:      Bool, Int, MyType
#   NAME_ID   lowercase-initial ident:      foo, my_fn, _
#   OPER      operator:                     + * -> => := :: |> ...
#   SEP       structural separator:         ,
#   L_PAR R_PAR   parentheses:              ( )
#   L_BRA R_BRA   brackets:                 [ ]
#   L_CUR R_CUR   curlies:                  { }
#   TAG       message / linear witness:     #Load, #Infix, #Undef
#   NUM       number literal:               42, 0.3, 0b01
#   STR       string literal:               "hello"
#   CHR       character literal:            0uA, 0u\n
#   COMMENT   line comment:                 // ...
#   INDENT DEDENT EOL EOF

#   `oper_id` (the operator as a value: (+) [->] {::}) is NOT a lexer token --- it
#   is a PARSER production ENC OPER ENC. It cannot be lexed, because the operator-
#   value vs container distinction is structural, not lexical: (+) is a value but
#   (+a) and (.a) are containers holding Prefix("+",a) / Prefix(".",a), and (+a)
#   has no interior space either. "Is there an atom after the operator" is a parse
#   question, so the parser owns it. See the oper_id / group productions.

#   ENC is the collective name for the six enclosure tokens
#   (L_PAR R_PAR L_BRA R_BRA L_CUR R_CUR). They are matched by construction in
#   `group`, so mismatched brackets like ( x ] are ungrammatical, not merely
#   ill-formed. The three KINDS (paren/bracket/curly) carry semantic meaning
#   (scope / list / set) resolved later; the token names stay purely syntactic.

# ── Classification ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   OPER      plays the spacing game --- fixity is positional (prefix / juxtafix /
#             postfix / infix from adjacency). Each operator carries exactly ONE
#             semantic bit: its associativity (left | right), read from the
#             primitive table at resolve, keyed on the SYMBOL, never on operand
#             type. There is NO precedence table.
#   SEP, ENC  structural: positional, never in operator position, spacing-
#             insensitive (adjacent _ is inert; absorbed by the lexer).
#   oper_id   a PARSER production, ENC OPER ENC --- the operator's NAME as a value,
#             an identifier alongside TYPE_ID and NAME_ID. It is disjoint from a
#             container because a container's `inner` needs an atom and oper_id's
#             content is a bare operator: (+) is only oper_id, (+a) is only a
#             container. Spacing inside is irrelevant; the distinction is whether
#             an atom follows. The bracket KIND selects a projection (see node
#             model): (op) the function, {op} the associativity, [op] the sig.
#   TAG       positional, and NOT an atom (cannot stand as a bare expression):
#               after TACET    -> the ONE linear witness it dispatches  [Tacet]
#               trailing expr  -> general annotation run (zero or more)  [Annot]
#             The two slots are non-interchangeable.
#   TACET     an atom, appearing anywhere an atom can (inline, in any expression).
#             Dispatches exactly ONE witness and ends right there --- no block, no
#             scope-header. Its influence is the enclosing Seq closure, exactly as
#             := introduces a name into the current closure and nothing more.
#             `tacet #Partial` = Tacet(#Partial). A further trailing tag is a
#             general annotation on the whole expression, not a second witness.

# ── Identifier family  (three names for three kinds of thing) ─────────────────
#   TYPE_ID  names a type       -> TypeId(name)          (lexer token)
#   NAME_ID  names a value/fn   -> NameId(name)          (lexer token)
#   oper_id  names an operator  -> OperId(bracket, op)   (parser production)
#   Each may bind (left of :=) or refer (standalone). `(+) := add #LeftAssoc`
#   binds the operator name exactly as `foo := ...` binds a value name. OperId
#   carries one extra field --- the bracket --- because an operator name is projected
#   three ways where a type/value name is not. (oper_id is a production, not a
#   token, because its recognition is structural --- see the token note above.)

# ── Tag families  (SEMANTIC, not structural --- the grammar sees only a tag) ──
#   Every TAG is structurally identical: it appears glued to `tacet` (the one
#   witness) or trailing an expression (the annotation run). The grammar does NOT
#   distinguish what a tag MEANS --- that is the analyser's job, routed by the tag's
#   identity through an open registry. Three families are expected:
#     witness    #Partial #Writes #Awaits    runtime; discharged linearly
#     directive  #Load #Module                analysis-time; consumed by analyser
#     fixity     #LeftAssoc #RightAssoc       analysis-time; sets operator table
#   The families are OPEN: a new tag is a new registry row, ZERO grammar change.
#   Keeping them semantic is the same choice as operators and witnesses being
#   analyser data, not enumerated productions --- one annotation slot serves all.

#   Module use-cases ride `annot` with no new syntax. A path is an ordinary
#   expression (e.g. `/` an operator over identifiers); a directive is a trailing
#   tag; their combination is Annot(path, [tag]):
#     stdlib/strings #Load            load module `strings` (named by its tail)
#     words := stdlib/strings #Load   load, bound to `words` (:= looser than the
#                                     tag, so #Load annotates the path, not the
#                                     binding --- correct by tier order)
#     my_project/.../this #Module     declare the current module's identity
#   Tacet has no import syntax and no module-declaration syntax as constructs:
#   it has paths (expressions) and directives (tags). The analyser decides
#   validity --- `path #Nonsense` PARSES (Annot) and is rejected semantically, not
#   structurally, which is the permissive-grammar principle working as intended.

# ── The tower  (loosest -> tightest; each nonterminal names one node) ─────────
#   seq     SEP-separated base sequence            Seq<()>
#   annot   trailing tags on a complete expr       Annot
#   infix   spaced operators                       Infix
#   apply   juxtaposition                          Apply
#   tight   adjacency                              Prefix | Juxtafix | Postfix
#   A run is flattened at construction; fixity is discharged there. A tree is
#   produced only on demand, by folding the flat run (see BINDING ORDER).

# ── Binding order  (no precedence; derived from associativity + tier) ─────────
#   Four levels, tightest -> loosest, each forced by structure, none declared:
#       tight       adjacency, single line, scalar operands        (tightest)
#       apply       juxtaposition; folds LEFT   (f x y = (f x) y)
#       left-assoc  spaced OPER, folds LEFT among themselves
#       right-assoc spaced OPER, folds RIGHT among themselves        (loosest)
#   Right-assoc is loosest because every outer-structure operator is right-
#   associative --- a head with an arbitrarily large tail (-> => := ::). That
#   shape is right-folding AND outermost; the two are one fact.
#     1 + 2 * 3          -> ((1 + 2) * 3)      same class (left), fold left
#     a + b -> c         -> ((a + b) -> c)     right (->) wraps left (+)
#     f :: Int -> Int    -> (f :: (Int -> Int))  :: and -> both right, fold right
#   RESOLVE is partition-then-fold, no climbing: right-assocs define the coarse
#   right-folded spine; each gap between them is a left-fold; apply and tight
#   fold within operands. Indentation is a SECOND, explicit grouping channel that
#   overrides the default fold (deeper = tighter / new closure --- see below).

# ── Seq operands by tier  (whether a newline can reach the operand) ───────────
#   A block is a sequence of lines. An operand can be a Seq iff its tier admits an
#   EOL at the operand boundary --- iff it can host a block:
#       apply     yes  -> Apply(seq)     block lines are the argument sequence
#       infix     yes  -> Infix(op, seq) dangling-op block is the right-operand seq
#       juxtafix  NO   -> Juxtafix(op,a,b) EOL breaks adjacency; a seq is UNWRITABLE
#   For apply and infix, deeper indentation opens a new closure / tighter group;
#   sibling lines are same-level operands. Juxtafix is confined to one line by
#   construction (`tight` contains no EOL), so its seq is impossible, not forbidden.
#   The tight tier hosts no EOL and no SEP, so ALL of its operators are scalar:
#       prefix  and  postfix  are UNARY   (decorate one atom, left / right)
#       juxtafix              is BINARY   (joins two atoms)
#   The `,`-terminator is never postfix --- commas are SEP (structural). Postfix is
#   x!, xs... : one operator on one atom. One axis (newline-at-boundary) cuts the
#   operator tower once: everything tight is scalar; everything spaced is a seq.

# ── Node model  (closed; every production lands in exactly one kind) ──────────
#   flat sequence + assoc bit          scalar tight            leaf / binder
#   ───────────────────────            ────────────            ─────────────
#   Seq(kind, [Expr])                  Juxtafix(op, a, b)      Tag(msg)
#   Annot(e, [Tag])                    Prefix(op, it)          OperId(brk, op)
#   Infix(op, seq)                     Postfix(op, it)         Tacet(witness)
#   Apply(seq)                                                 TypeId  NameId
#                                                              NUM STR CHR
#   Seq does NOT collapse; each Seq is a closure. Infix/Apply carry their OWN flat
#   list, never a Seq (else f a b would read as a closure boundary). Tag is a leaf
#   only inside Tacet/Annot, never a bare atom. Tacet is an atom carrying exactly
#   ONE witness Tag; it dispatches that witness at its position and ends there,
#   scoped by the enclosing Seq (no body). OperId carries the bracket kind, which
#   selects the projection: (op) function, {op} associativity, [op] signature. No
#   node carries a payload its tier cannot produce; no tier produces a shape
#   without a node. Throw any construct at the four kinds --- it lands, and none
#   forces a fifth.

# ── Sequences and closures ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   Every ENC opens a closure. Seq does NOT collapse: Seq[Seq[a,b],c] is distinct
#   from Seq[a,b,c] --- a new sequence is a new context. Closures are OPAQUE by
#   construction, but TRANSPARENT whenever their environment extension is the
#   identity --- i.e. they bind no name visible in their body. Then the closure's
#   value is its body in the enclosing environment, so (a) = a --- always, because
#   (a) introduces no a. A binder in the body (:=, a tacet witness, a lambda
#   parameter, a binding =>) makes the closure opaque. Transparency is decidable
#   structurally, from the subtree alone, and is stable under any change outside
#   the closure.
#   Corollary: grouping-for-precedence is always transparent; only binding
#   brackets carry weight. One construct; the two roles are extension = id or not.

# ── Binding  (no shadowing of the visible) ────────────────────────────────────
#   Tacet is immutable: := binds, never assigns. A := binding of n is ILLEGAL iff
#   n is currently reachable (bound earlier in the same Seq, or in an enclosing
#   closure). Visibility = the enclosing Seq subtree. Siblings thread forward;
#   inner sees outer; one bind per name per Seq. Enforced outer-to-inner during
#   resolve: push a scope frame per closure, insert per :=, POP on closure exit
#   (release is free). O(N) --- one hash probe per binding, fused into resolve.
#     Seq("()", [ (x := 0), (x := 1), 2 ])   legal  (each () a fresh closure)
#     Seq("()", [ x := 0,   x := 1,   2 ])   illegal (same closure, x rebound)
#   The check tests BINDINGS against visible scope; it never resolves references,
#   so recursion (is := recursive?) never enters it --- a body's `f` is a use.

# ── Scope  (no production needed) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#   A trailing OPER with an empty right operand + EOL block resolves to a Scope:
#   `f :=` then a block splices the block into the dangling operand.

# ── Lexer obligations  (structural, unstatable in productions) ────────────────
#   L1  Line-final _ is stripped before EOL. A trailing space must never survive
#       to distinguish postfix-complete from dangling-infix.
#   L2  _ adjacent to SEP or ENC is absorbed:  a , b -> a SEP b.
#   L3  TACET lexes as the keyword, never NAME_ID.
#   L4  // is comment-only, by maximal munch --- never two / operators. The _
#       before a COMMENT is pure formatting and is absorbed: `x  // note` lexes
#       as `x COMMENT`, no _ between. So `line` needs no space between seq and
#       COMMENT; the gap is a lexer concern, like L2, not a structural one.

#   (There is no lexer obligation for operator-values: (+) etc. is the parser
#   production oper_id, because the value/container distinction is structural,
#   not lexical --- see the token note near the top.)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


program
    = { line } EOF
    ;

line
    = [ seq ] [ COMMENT ] EOL [ block ]
    ;

block
    = INDENT { line } DEDENT
    ;

seq
    = annot { [ _ ] SEP [ _ ] annot }
    ;

annot
    = infix { _ TAG }
    ;

infix
    = apply { _ OPER [ _ apply ] }
    ;

apply
    = tight { _ tight }
    ;

tight
    = [ OPER ] atom { OPER atom } [ OPER ]
    ;

atom
    = TYPE_ID
    | NAME_ID
    | NUM
    | STR
    | CHR
    | tacet
    | oper_id
    | group
    ;

tacet
    = TACET _ TAG
    ;

oper_id
    = L_PAR OPER R_PAR
    | L_BRA OPER R_BRA
    | L_CUR OPER R_CUR
    ;

group
    = L_PAR inner R_PAR
    | L_BRA inner R_BRA
    | L_CUR inner R_CUR
    ;

inner
    = [ _ ] [ seq [ _ ] ]
    | EOL block
    ;
